1
00:00:00,165 --> 00:00:01,015
<u>Dependency Injection</u>

2
00:00:02,321 --> 00:00:03,921
<i>Why is Dependency Injection needed?</i>

3
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:09,450
DI passes dependencies into a class from the outside, reducing coupling and making testing easier.

4
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:12,970
It also centralizes object creation and lifecycle management.

5
00:00:14,481 --> 00:00:16,651
<i>What is the difference between DI and Service Locator?</i>

6
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:21,640
With DI, dependencies are passed explicitly through a constructor or method.

7
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:25,850
With Service Locator, a class asks a global container for dependencies itself,

8
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:27,120
which makes them less visible.

9
00:00:28,638 --> 00:00:29,278
<i>What is Hilt?</i>

10
00:00:30,782 --> 00:00:34,752
Hilt is a DI framework built on top of Dagger with ready-made Android lifecycle integration.

11
00:00:35,462 --> 00:00:40,352
It simplifies dependency graphs, injection into Activity, Fragment, and ViewModel, and scope management.

12
00:00:41,969 --> 00:00:44,289
<i>What is the difference between @Binds and @Provides?</i>

13
00:00:45,912 --> 00:00:49,652
@Binds connects an interface to an implementation that can already be created by DI.

14
00:00:50,502 --> 00:00:55,562
@Provides is used when an object must be created manually, for example Retrofit, Room, or an external SDK.

15
00:00:57,081 --> 00:00:58,211
<i>What is a scope in DI?</i>

16
00:00:59,715 --> 00:01:03,015
A scope defines how long one instance is reused inside the dependency graph.

17
00:01:03,725 --> 00:01:07,235
It should match the real lifecycle of the object rather than defaulting everything to Singleton.

18
00:01:08,761 --> 00:01:10,451
<i>Why should not everything be a singleton?</i>

19
00:01:11,995 --> 00:01:16,255
A singleton increases an object's lifetime, can keep unnecessary state, and makes testing harder.

20
00:01:16,965 --> 00:01:20,715
Only truly application-wide stateless services or resources should usually be long-lived.
